    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.lexicographical_topological_sort -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.lexicographical_topological_sort.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.lexicographical_topological_sort()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the lexicographically smallest topological order of a directed acyclic\n    graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.lexicographical_topological_sort()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the lexicographically smallest topological order of a directed acyclic\n    graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.lexicographical_topological_sort()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find the lexicographically smallest topological order of a directed acyclic\n  graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind the lexicographically smallest topological order of a directed acyclic graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L66942-L67060){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.lexicographical_topological_sort(key=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Lexicographical topological sort](../assets/graphs/lexicographical-topological-sort.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Getting dressed as a DAG: shirt before tie, trousers before belt and shoes, socks before shoes. Numbered badges give the order returned, which always takes the alphabetically first item whose prerequisites are done: 1 shirt, 2 socks, 3 tie, 4 trousers, 5 belt, 6 shoes. The card lists the same order.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nMany orders respect the edges of a DAG. This method returns a canonical one: at every\nstep it takes, among the nodes whose predecessors are all placed, the smallest by\n`key`. The result is deterministic and readable (tasks in alphabetical order whenever\nthe dependencies allow), which is useful for reproducible build orders, stable course\nschedules, and comparing orders across runs.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[key]{.doc-parameter-name}[=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op}[None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional function `key(node_id)` giving the sort key. If `None` (default), nodes are\n    ordered by label (as strings; ties by node ID).\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node IDs in order.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is undirected or has a cycle.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nKahn's algorithm with a priority queue of available nodes, $O((V + E) \\log V)$. Matches\nNetworkX's `lexicographical_topological_sort` with the same key.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~topological_sort`) \n\n:   One topological ordering.\n\n[](`~all_topological_sorts`) \n\n:   Every topological ordering.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOf the orders that respect the edges, this one takes the smallest available label at\neach step, so `B` comes before `C`, which is free from the start:\n\n::: {#c181806b .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"D\")], directed=True\n)\norder = graph.lexicographical_topological_sort()\n\n[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in order]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A schedule by course level\n\n`courses()` holds the courses of a made-up computer science program, with an arrow from\neach course to every course that requires it. Ordering by label puts every CS course it\ncan before any math course, so Calculus I comes ninth:\n\n::: {#02adba84 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncourses = ec.courses()\n\n[courses.get_label_from_id(n)\n for n in courses.lexicographical_topological_sort()]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['CS101',\n 'CS102',\n 'CS210',\n 'CS220',\n 'CS250',\n 'CS310',\n 'CS320',\n 'CS410',\n 'MATH101',\n 'MATH102',\n 'MATH150',\n 'CS201',\n 'MATH210',\n 'STAT201',\n 'CS350',\n 'CS360',\n 'CS460']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `key=` the course level, the order takes the 100-level courses first, then the\n200-level ones, and so on, still never taking a course before its prerequisites:\n\n::: {#fc703671 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nlevel = dict(courses.get_node_df().select(\"id\", \"level\").iter_rows())\norder = courses.lexicographical_topological_sort(key=level.get)\n\n[courses.get_label_from_id(n) for n in order]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n['CS101',\n 'CS102',\n 'MATH101',\n 'MATH102',\n 'MATH150',\n 'CS201',\n 'CS210',\n 'CS220',\n 'CS250',\n 'MATH210',\n 'STAT201',\n 'CS310',\n 'CS320',\n 'CS350',\n 'CS360',\n 'CS410',\n 'CS460']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#c06a9f01 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncourses.set_node_attrs(\n    \"position\", {n: i + 1 for i, n in enumerate(order)}\n)\nstages = dict(enumerate(courses.topological_generations()))\ncourses.layout_multipartite(subset_key=stages).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"position\", color_scale=\"sequential\",\n    label_by=\"label\", height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-d29233f2:#143d72;--ect-d06136e8:#5f8cc5;--ect-bedcb8ea:#6e9cd5;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-73a00b98:#3a649b;--ect-ff6c91af:#244d83;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-5dd4fa0a:#5683bb;--ect-7e8fe88c:#6694cd;--ect-eb0fa70b:#325c92;--ect-ff1ea111:#1c447a;--ect-fd7dd8f9:#7eaee7;--ect-c26f65a1:#77a6de;--ect-f572b836:#4f7bb3;--ect-8133927f:#416ca3;--ect-764eb3cb:#2b548b;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-7e01d800:#0e2a4f;--ect-63d20321:#426289;--ect-41288e7c:#4d6d95;--ect-17801347:#325177;--ect-1bcffdb4:#28466c;--ect-1b75f7cc:#19355b;--ect-6970a796:#5d7fa7;--ect-bf50105f:#09254a;--ect-55d85fd9:#3c5b82;--ect-8a54ada3:#47678f;--ect-47a5e22d:#234066;--ect-806d374d:#132f55;--ect-f991b38f:#5879a1;--ect-7ac0b982:#53749b;--ect-a5899a45:#37567d;--ect-3838da4c:#2d4b72;--ect-06730740:#1e3a61;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(